1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. d3 { The so-called anti-Berlin, which was long considered completetely harmless but has been gaining more and more popularity in recent years due to futility of White's attempts in the Berlin Endgame. } Bc5 { In this chapter we will focus on 5.Nc3, 5.Nbd2 and 5.O-O while 5.c3 and 5.Bxc6 are covered later. } 5. O-O Nd4 $1 { I like this move, taking advantage of the lack of c3 move much more than the passive 5.... d6. This knight exchange is advantageous to Black and I see no reason why White would be the one playing for the edge here. In fact, we will see that Black can take over the initiative quite easily if White is not cautious. Note that 5...0-0 just loses a pawn to 6.Bxc6 dxc6 7. Nxe5. } 6. Nxd4 Bxd4 7. c3 Bb6 8. d4 { May lead to quick simplifications but we have a nice little novelty. } a6 $5 { (The main move is 8. ..0-0 and after 9.dxe5 Nxe4 10.Nd2 d5! 11.Nxe4 dxe4 12. Qxd8 Rxd8 the position is drawish.) } 9. Bd3 { -KEY- } d6 { All Black's pieces are well placed and he has nothing to worry about. } 
