[White "6. Ruy Lopez: Anti-Berlin - 4.d3 with 5.0-0 & others"]
[Black "Anti-Berlin 4.d3 & 5.0-0 non critical example"]
[Result "*"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. d3 { The so-called anti-Berlin, which was long considered completetely harmless but has been gaining more and more popularity in recent years due to futility of White's attempts in the Berlin Endgame. } Bc5 { In this chapter we will focus on 5.Nc3, 5.Nbd2 and 5.O-O while 5.c3 and 5.Bxc6 are covered later. } 5. O-O Nd4 $1 { I like this move, taking advantage of the lack of c3 move much more than the passive 5.... d6. This knight exchange is advantageous to Black and I see no reason why White would be the one playing for the edge here. In fact, we will see that Black can take over the initiative quite easily if White is not cautious. Note that 5...0-0 just loses a pawn to 6.Bxc6 dxc6 7. Nxe5. } 6. Nxd4 Bxd4 7. c3 Bb6 8. Na3 { White plans to play Nc4 to chase away the bishop from the optimal diagonal. -KEY- } c6 9. Ba4 d6 10. Bc2 { Completely non-critical. Black can pretty much play as he pleases. Wesley So chose an ambitious plan and gained an upper hand against Fabiano Caruana. } Be6 11. Qe2 ( 11. Kh1 $2 { would be a blunder in view of } Ng4 $1 { and 12...Qh4. } ) 
h6 12. Kh1 g5 { Black prevents f4 once and for all. } 13. Nc4 Bc7 14. Ne3 d5 { Black is going to castle long and will have promising play in the centre and on the kingside. } 15. Re1 Qe7 16. a4 O-O-O 17. Rb1 d4 18. Nf1 { This was Caruana-So, Norway Chess 2018 Now after } g4 $17 { Black would be much better. } 
 *
